# =============================================================================
# Walter-OS — environment variables (template)
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in real values. Or load
# secrets from Infisical at runtime:
#   infisical run --env=dev -- bash -c '<command>'
# That way no secret ever lives in a file on disk.
#
# This file is the MINIMUM set to get Walter-OS running end-to-end. Phase-specific
# extras (Singer taps, Postgres analytics, etc.) live in apps/control-tower/.env.example
# and setup/walter-host/services/<service>/.env files.
#
# Conventions:
#   - All real secrets via Infisical (https://infisical.com) — gitignored
#   - Tokens that begin with `your-...` are placeholders → replace
#   - macOS Keychain integration: see scripts/secrets-keychain-init.sh
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 0) Deployment domain — required by many service configs
# =============================================================================
# All service subdomains (plane.${WALTER_DOMAIN}, secrets.${WALTER_DOMAIN}, etc.)
# are derived from this single value. Set it to the domain you own.
WALTER_DOMAIN=example.com
# Admin email for Cloudflare Access policies and first-run admin forms
WALTER_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# Telegram bot handle for the Walter alerting bot (without @)
WALTER_TELEGRAM_BOT_HANDLE=your_walter_bot
# Telegram bot handle for the OpenClaw assistant bot (without @)
WALTER_OPENCLAW_BOT_HANDLE=your_openclaw_bot
# Tailscale user identifier (the part before @ in ACL rules)
WALTER_ADMIN_TAILSCALE_USER=your-tailscale-user
# Matrix/Synapse operator username (used in deploy.sh success message and initial user creation)
# Default: admin — change to your preferred Matrix handle
# WALTER_MATRIX_USER=admin

# =============================================================================
# 1) Walter-OS core paths
# =============================================================================

# Where this repo is cloned (used by bin/walter + bin/walter-os to find scripts)
WALTER_OS_HOME=$HOME/.local/share/walter-os  # or wherever you cloned the repo

# Per-operator config dir (cron mode, gate.lock, mode.json, audit reports)
WALTER_CONFIG=$HOME/.config/walter-os

# Optional override for agent-approvals.yml (standing approvals)
# WALTER_STANDING_APPROVALS=$WALTER_CONFIG/agent-approvals.yml

# Fork support: override the GitHub repo used for update-check in `walter-os version`.
# Set to YOUR fork's org/repo (or to the upstream you track). The binary
# does NOT ship a hardcoded default here. When this variable is unset,
# `walter-os version` derives the repo from `git remote get-url origin`
# of the walter-os checkout — falling back to skipping the update check
# (with a one-line INFO message) when no usable github.com remote is
# found. See cmd_version in bin/walter-os for the resolution order.
# Example: WALTER_OS_UPDATE_REPO=your-org/walter-os
# WALTER_OS_UPDATE_REPO=


# =============================================================================
# 2) LLM provider — pick ONE primary, plus optional fallbacks
# =============================================================================
# Walter-OS routes LLM calls through scripts/agents/lib/llm.sh which checks:
#   1. LITELLM_BASE_URL + LITELLM_API_KEY  (preferred — budget caps, telemetry)
#   2. ANTHROPIC_ENTERPRISE_KEY            (when WALTER_AGENT_CONTEXT=work)
#   3. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY                   (personal context fallback)

# Preferred: LiteLLM gateway (self-hosted on walter-vm)
# Configure providers in setup/walter-host/services/litellm/config.yaml
LITELLM_BASE_URL=http://litellm:4000
LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-your-litellm-master-key

# Direct Anthropic (fallback if LiteLLM unavailable)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-personal-key

# Enterprise Anthropic (used when WALTER_AGENT_CONTEXT=work, e.g. your company repos)
ANTHROPIC_ENTERPRISE_KEY=sk-ant-your-enterprise-key

# Optional: direct fallback keys (used when LiteLLM unavailable or bypassed)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-openai-key
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=your-google-genai-key
# GEMINI_API_KEY=AI...          # required for gemini, gemini-2.5-pro, gemini-2.5-flash aliases

# REQUIRED if using the assistant profile (claude-sub / claude-sub-opus aliases via claude-code-router)
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# CCR_APIKEY=

# === Additional LiteLLM-supported providers ===
# Set only what you use; LiteLLM warns at startup for unconfigured providers — aliases fail at call time, not load time.
# Full list of 100+ providers: https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/providers

# Groq (fast inference, free tier available)
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...

# DeepSeek (cost-effective reasoning)
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...

# Mistral (French, GDPR-friendly)
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=...

# Cohere (enterprise RAG-optimized)
# COHERE_API_KEY=...

# Azure OpenAI (enterprise GPT-4 deployment)
# AZURE_API_KEY=...
# AZURE_API_BASE=https://your-resource.openai.azure.com
# AZURE_API_VERSION=2024-02-01

# AWS Bedrock (Claude/Llama via AWS, requires IAM role or keys)
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
# AWS_REGION_NAME=us-east-1

# Google Vertex AI (enterprise Gemini)
# Auth via ADC: set GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS to a service-account JSON path.
# LiteLLM reads it automatically — do NOT pass it as api_key in config.yaml.
# VERTEXAI_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id
# VERTEXAI_LOCATION=us-central1
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/path/to/service-account.json

# xAI Grok
# XAI_API_KEY=xai-...

# Perplexity (search-grounded answers)
# PERPLEXITYAI_API_KEY=pplx-...

# Together AI (open-source model hosting)
# TOGETHERAI_API_KEY=...

# DeepInfra (GPU inference)
# DEEPINFRA_API_KEY=...

# Replicate (model hosting)
# REPLICATE_API_KEY=r8_...

# =============================================================================
# 3) Local LLM (optional — privacy-sensitive workloads, e.g. medical records, PHI)
# =============================================================================
# Configure Ollama or vLLM endpoint here. Walter-OS routes PHI-tagged tasks
# locally (never to external APIs).
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
# VLLM_BASE_URL=http://vllm:8000
# VLLM_API_KEY=your-vllm-api-key-if-set
#
# Local model selection: the Ollama model is hardcoded to llama3.1 in
# setup/litellm/config.yaml. To change it, edit that file and restart the
# LiteLLM container. The vLLM model is configured on the vLLM process itself.


# =============================================================================
# 4) Plane (project management)
# =============================================================================
# Self-hosted at plane.${WALTER_DOMAIN} (or cloud at app.plane.so).
# Agents use Plane as the durable task queue.
# Constructed from WALTER_DOMAIN. Example: https://plane.example.com/api/v1
# Note: dotenv does not expand ${...} — set this to the literal URL for your domain.
PLANE_API_URL=
PLANE_API_TOKEN=your-plane-api-token
PLANE_WORKSPACE=walter-os
PLANE_PROJECT=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000


# =============================================================================
# 5) Secrets manager — Infisical
# =============================================================================
# Self-hosted at secrets.${WALTER_DOMAIN} (or cloud at app.infisical.com).
# Create a Machine Identity per device. Universal Auth flow.
INFISICAL_API_URL=https://secrets.${WALTER_DOMAIN}
INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID=your-machine-identity-client-id
INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET=your-machine-identity-client-secret
# At runtime, scripts/secrets-keychain-init.sh wraps these in macOS Keychain
# and exports INFISICAL_TOKEN per session (12h lifetime).


# =============================================================================
# 6) Telegram alerting (alerts.sh tiers: info/warn/critical/panic)
# =============================================================================
# Create a bot via @BotFather, then add it to your chat to get the chat ID.
WALTER_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:your-bot-token-from-botfather
WALTER_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=-1001234567890


# =============================================================================
# 7) GitHub (PR creation, releases, audit lookups)
# =============================================================================
# Use a fine-grained PAT scoped to specific repos when possible.
GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_your-fine-grained-pat

# Canonical provider identifier written by `walter providers configure`.
# Values: github | forgejo | gitlab
# Scripts use this to avoid inferring the provider from which token is set.
# GIT_PROVIDER=github


# =============================================================================
# 8) Forgejo (self-hosted git, optional — replaces or supplements GitHub)
# =============================================================================
# FORGEJO_URL=https://git.${WALTER_DOMAIN}
# FORGEJO_TOKEN=your-forgejo-pat


# =============================================================================
# 9) Hetzner Cloud (provisioning Walter-VM, optional — high-risk profile)
# =============================================================================
# Only set when actively provisioning. Use a read-only token by default;
# mint write-scoped tokens for the duration of provisioning, then revoke.
# HCLOUD_TOKEN=your-hetzner-cloud-token


# =============================================================================
# 10) Phase F observability (Prometheus + Grafana embed)
# =============================================================================
# Service account token for Control Tower iframe embeds.
# GRAFANA_URL=https://grafana.${WALTER_DOMAIN}
# GRAFANA_SA_TOKEN=your-grafana-service-account-token


# =============================================================================
# 11) Phase M lessons DB (Postgres, optional — see council-v2-prereqs.md)
# =============================================================================
# LESSONS_DB_URL=postgresql://lessons_writer:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/walter_lessons


# =============================================================================
# 12) Phase V analytics / marketing core (see devrel-analytics-stack.md)
# =============================================================================
# ANALYTICS_DB_URL=postgresql://analytics_writer:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/walter_analytics
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=your-google-cloud-youtube-data-api-v3-key
# PLAUSIBLE_API_KEY=your-plausible-api-key
# PLAUSIBLE_SITE_ID=your-site-id

# Postiz — required (no defaults; compose will refuse to start without these)
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 16
POSTIZ_PG_PASS=
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
POSTIZ_JWT_SECRET=


# =============================================================================
# 13) Branch-flow-guard — manual PR enforcement (optional)
# =============================================================================
# When WALTER_MANUAL_PR_REMOTE_PATTERN is set, the branch-flow-guard hook
# blocks automated `gh pr create` on remotes whose URL contains this substring.
# The agent prints the command instead; the operator runs it manually.
# Leave unset (default) to disable enforcement.
#
# WALTER_MANUAL_PR_REMOTE_PATTERN=acme-corp  # enforce manual gh pr create on remotes matching this substring
# WALTER_MANUAL_PR_OVERRIDE_FLAG=--allow-acme-pr  # custom bypass flag (default: --allow-manual-pr)


# =============================================================================
# 14) Agent runtime context (set by walter-os agents run, do not export globally)
# =============================================================================
# WALTER_AGENT_NAME=reviewer
# WALTER_AGENT_CONTEXT=personal     # personal | work
# WALTER_AGENT_PLANE_ISSUE=ISSUE-123
# WALTER_LLM_RAW_FILE=/tmp/agent-llm-raw.json  # written by run.sh for token attribution

# =============================================================================
# 15) Testing / CI — mock LLM mode
# =============================================================================
# Set WALTER_LLM_MOCK_FILE to a JSON file containing {"mock_response": "..."}
# to bypass real LLM calls. Used in bats tests and CI pipelines. When set,
# llm_invoke_or_mock emits a [WARN] to stderr so mock mode is never silent.
# NEVER set this in production; it causes every AI command to return fake data.
#
# Example (tests/fixtures/mock-ask.json):
#   {"mock_response": "mock-ask-response"}
#
# WALTER_LLM_MOCK_FILE=/path/to/mock-response.json
